  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 316 AM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\n***** ADDITIONAL STORMS WEDNESDAY INTO WEDNESDAY NIGHT *****\n\nDepending on how things evolve tonight, we could have storms ongoing \nduring the morning commute tomorrow, with the effective boundary \nfueling continued storms throughout the day. However, the highest \nchance for storms over the local area will once again be during the \nevening when another shortwave digs across the Upper Ohio Valley, \nproviding a lifting mechanism and better (30-40 kt) deep layer shear \nto sustain convection. Uncertainty surrounds how much of a severe \nrisk will accompany this activity given potential for widespread \nclouds and early-day storms which could limit instability, but if we \nsee enough breaks of sunshine the environment would certainly be \nfavorable, once again, for one or more MCSs capable of damaging \nwinds and torrential rainfall leading to localized flash flooding. \nAt this time, SPC has areas west of I-65 outlooked in a level 2 of 5 \n(slight risk) for severe weather.\n\n***** THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY *****\n\nGlobal deterministic models and their respective ensembles suggest \nridging should build over the Midwest and Ohio Valley into the \nweekend, suggesting a downward trend in precipitation chances and \ncontinued warm, muggy conditions. However, particularly Thursday and \nFriday, we remain preciously close to the ring of fire - the \nnortheast periphery of the ridge where the best overlap of \ninstability and deep layer shear will favor MCS activity. Storm \nchances, around 40-50% northeast of a Jasper, Indiana to Somerset, \nKentucky line Thursday into Friday, will finally drop below 20% by \nSaturday.",
